    verification oneCorpusPerProcessReviewGate : Test { :>> id = "5f47b32a-fab4-4e35-ac87-5f9b96f80134"; :>> title = "Sprint 600 review gate — three passes per D0172"; :>> createdAt = "2026-09-07"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::inspect; :>> procedureText = "THREE QUESTIONS (D0172). Can a cached read be wrong? Only when a file is rewritten to the same length within the same mtime tick as the read that cached it - and that entry is not served for two seconds, so the window is the mtime resolution against a two-second bar, the same bar git's index trusts. Is the output the same? Identical guard verdicts (66 PASS, 0 FAIL, diff empty modulo timing) and byte-identical orient on this tree. What is NOT claimed: that a file opened once per process gains anything (write paths, status and serve stay on std::fs); that the wall time transfers (it is this host's Defender open cost); that git is now cheap - `git x44 in 6.4-9.1 s` under parallel contention is the residual, and it is what dcGateAnswersFromItsReceipt and dcGitReadsStayInProcess judge next."; }
    // RAN: cargo clippy --release -p keel-cli --all-targets clean; cargo test --release -p keel-cli --lib 407 passed; keel guard 8293/7176 ms -> 6523/4587/5562 ms, stdout identical modulo ms lines; keel orient 2106/1847 -> 1645/1554 ms byte-identical; keel validate . 682 clean
